# Alien Number Systems: Counting on a Grid with a Single Complex Base

Base ten hides four quiet conventions: an integer base, a digit alphabet, a minus sign, and a radix point. Relax them and a wider world of number systems appears. This page explores the most striking one: the **complex base $\beta = i - 1$**, which with only the digits $0$ and $1$ names *every* point of the two-dimensional grid of Gaussian integers $a + b\,i$ exactly once, with no sign and no separate imaginary digit.

## Why one complex base reaches everywhere

The secret is that the powers $\beta^0, \beta^1, \beta^2, \dots$ do not march along a single ray as $1, 10, 100, \dots$ do; they spiral through the plane, pointing in all directions. Move the slider below and watch them fan out.

